VERDICT: PASS -- all three actions behave distinctly and correctly.
===========================================================================
  Source MAC 0000.0200.0600 (exact mask), destination any; one action per
  run, applied to port2.0.2 as access-group <n>.

  | list | action         | forwarded | expected | hits |
  |------|----------------|-----------|----------|------|
  | 4000 | copy-to-cpu    | 10/10     | forward  | 10   |
  | 4001 | send-to-cpu    |  0/10     | drop     | 10   |
  | 4002 | copy-to-mirror | 10/10     | forward  | 10   |
  (baseline for all three: 10/10)

  The three actions are demonstrably DIFFERENT, which is the point of the
  case: copy-* leaves hardware forwarding intact while send-to-cpu removes
  the packet from the forwarding path. All three matched 10 packets, so none
  is a silent no-op.

NOT VERIFIED (bench limitation, same as 943.log): delivery to the CPU queue
and to the mirror port were not observed directly. `show plat count sdma` was
not read, and the mirror destination port3.0.2 has no cable so it cannot
transmit. The forwarding-side behaviour above is what distinguishes the
actions and it is measured; the delivery side is inferred.
